WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.273
Cross-Text Connections

00:00:02.653 --> 00:00:05.703
One sentence per author. Then compare.

00:00:06.903 --> 00:00:07.994
How to spot it

00:00:08.394 --> 00:00:10.016
Text 1 and Text 2

00:00:10.566 --> 00:00:14.492
The question says “how would the author of Text 2 respond”

00:00:15.042 --> 00:00:18.055
It’s testing: tracking two viewpoints at once

00:00:19.255 --> 00:00:20.021
The method

00:00:20.421 --> 00:00:23.146
Step 1: Summarise Text 1

00:00:23.426 --> 00:00:24.532
One sentence.

00:00:25.082 --> 00:00:27.299
Step 2: Summarise Text 2

00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:28.685
One sentence.

00:00:29.235 --> 00:00:32.022
Step 3: Name the relationship

00:00:32.302 --> 00:00:35.504
Agree, disagree, partly agree, or adds a limit.

00:00:36.054 --> 00:00:39.083
Step 4: Answer the exact point

00:00:39.363 --> 00:00:41.294
What would Text 2 say about THAT?

00:00:41.844 --> 00:00:42.939
In a few words

00:00:43.089 --> 00:00:45.579
Summarise both. Name the link.

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:48.238
Relationship clues

00:00:48.638 --> 00:00:50.268
“It is true that…, but…”

00:00:50.548 --> 00:00:51.703
Partly agrees.

00:00:52.253 --> 00:00:53.908
“Whatever X intended…”

00:00:54.188 --> 00:00:56.618
Concedes one point, shifts to another.

00:00:57.168 --> 00:00:58.311
Usually measured

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.878
Text 2 rarely rejects everything.

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:02.928
Facts vs cause

00:01:03.208 --> 00:01:05.841
Often agree on the data, disagree on why.

00:01:07.041 --> 00:01:08.102
Let’s try one

00:01:08.502 --> 00:01:09.349
Text 1

00:01:09.599 --> 00:01:14.934
Studies show that students who play musical instruments tend to earn higher math scores than students

00:01:14.934 --> 00:01:21.646
who don’t. Clearly, learning music improves mathematical ability, and schools should expand their

00:01:21.646 --> 00:01:23.742
music programs for this reason.

00:01:23.992 --> 00:01:24.832
Text 2

00:01:25.082 --> 00:01:28.769
It is true that young musicians often excel at math.

00:01:28.769 --> 00:01:34.299
But families who can afford instruments and lessons also tend to provide other advantages,

00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:37.329
such as tutoring and quiet study spaces.

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:42.995
Without studies that account for these factors, we cannot say that music itself causes better math

00:01:42.995 --> 00:01:43.871
performance.

00:01:44.421 --> 00:01:49.845
Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim made in Text

00:01:49.845 --> 00:01:50.284
1?

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:54.279
Text 1: music causes better math scores.

00:01:54.829 --> 00:02:00.945
Text 2: it’s true that musicians score higher, but we can’t say music is the cause.

00:02:01.495 --> 00:02:06.034
Text 2 agrees on the pattern, but questions the cause. Choice B.

00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:08.303
Common traps

00:02:08.703 --> 00:02:10.039
Total disagreement

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:13.058
Usually they clash on one specific point.

00:02:13.608 --> 00:02:14.502
Wrong author

00:02:14.782 --> 00:02:16.393
Mixing up who said what.

00:02:17.593 --> 00:02:19.266
Cross-Text Connections

00:02:19.546 --> 00:02:22.035
Summarise both. Name the link.

00:02:22.585 --> 00:02:23.165
Spot it

00:02:23.365 --> 00:02:28.145
Text 1 + Text 2: “how would the author of Text 2 respond”

00:02:28.695 --> 00:02:29.401
Steps

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:32.088
1. Sum up Text 1 in one sentence

00:02:32.368 --> 00:02:34.781
2. Sum up Text 2 in one sentence

00:02:35.061 --> 00:02:36.264
3. Name the link

00:02:36.544 --> 00:02:38.703
4. Answer the exact point asked

00:02:39.253 --> 00:02:39.978
Remember

00:02:40.178 --> 00:02:43.037
“It is true that…, but…” = partly agrees

00:02:43.317 --> 00:02:45.604
Text 2 rarely rejects everything

00:02:45.884 --> 00:02:48.659
Often: same facts, different cause

00:02:49.209 --> 00:02:49.650
Trap

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.693
Assuming total disagreement

00:02:52.443 --> 00:02:55.861
